{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2868 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "08daee9fdc5f0d6f7319805f0735f2b72bf282ac0e9b7538cade582310f89448" :edb/db-before-t 2867 :edb/db-after-t 2868 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536180 50 #inst "2026-10-09T18:30:14.193Z" 13194139536180 true] [17592186184095 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387544 13194139536180 true] [17592186184096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387551 13194139536180 true] [17592186184096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387559 13194139536180 true] [17592186356492 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083206/assets/19d9904c5236534ee6b4f176d760268df397f644d4ea7016d1d35c4b5db6e413.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={g}^{″}(x)$ of the function $g(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-4,$ $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ $x=2,$ and $x=4,$ as shown above. The intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up are\n\n$(-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$),\\,(${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$),\\,(${{field-4}}$,4).$" 13194139536180 true] [17592186356492 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/assets/19d9904c5236534ee6b4f176d760268df397f644d4ea7016d1d35c4b5db6e413.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={g}^{″}(x)$ of the function $g(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-4,$ $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ $x=2,$ and $x=4,$ as shown above. The intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up are\n\n$(-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$),\\,(${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$),\\,(${{field-4}}$,4).$" 13194139536180 false] [17592186356493 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387558 13194139536180 true] [17592186387544 1017 #edb/ref 17592186387545 13194139536180 true] [17592186387544 1089 "q-22826" 13194139536180 true] [17592186387544 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536180 true] [17592186387544 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x}$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$" 13194139536180 true] [17592186387544 1113 #uuid "b6aac796-5f94-58f0-b828-88b8f756ca51" 13194139536180 true] [17592186387544 1145 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & >0 \\\\ 2 & >\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {x}^{3} & >1 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that $x>0.$\n\nHence, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ when $x>1.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $2-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}<0,$ which has the solution $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II only.\"" 13194139536180 true] [17592186387545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387546 13194139536180 true] [17592186387545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387547 13194139536180 true] [17592186387545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387548 13194139536180 true] [17592186387545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387549 13194139536180 true] [17592186387545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387550 13194139536180 true] [17592186387545 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536180 true] [17592186387545 1041 "selection" 13194139536180 true] [17592186387545 1104 #edb/ref 17592186387550 13194139536180 true] [17592186387545 1110 #uuid "21ebbd00-a8f1-5c5d-aee6-4c58a601bce8" 13194139536180 true] [17592186387546 1034 "I and III only" 13194139536180 true] [17592186387546 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536180 true] [17592186387546 1111 #uuid "6f408129-e07b-5307-91be-0187e2e6b467" 13194139536180 true] [17592186387547 1034 "I only" 13194139536180 true] [17592186387547 1103 #edb/ref 1759218604